# Prompt Playbook: Building & Testing the 37 Bond Alpha Factors

Copy-paste prompts, in dependency order. Each prompt is self-contained but assumes the artifacts of earlier prompts exist. Attach `bond_alpha_factors.md` (or the PDF) to every session so the factor definitions F1–F37 and Algorithms 1–8 are in context.

Conventions used below:
- **[DATA]** = your data root, **[UNIVERSE]** = your bond universe filter (e.g. USD IG, >$250m outstanding).
- Every code prompt ends with the same three demands: unit tests, a smoke run on a small sample, and a written list of assumptions the code made. Keep those in — they catch most silent errors.
- Run Phase 0 once; Phases 1–3 are per-family; Phase 4–5 once.

---

## Phase 0 — Environment and data

### P0.1 — Project scaffold
> Create a Python project `bond_alpha` for computing and testing corporate-bond alpha factors. Structure: `data/` (raw, clean, bars), `factors/` (one module per family), `estimation/` (fitted models), `evaluation/`, `tests/`, `config.yaml`. Use pandas + numpy + scipy + statsmodels + scikit-learn only; polars optional for the tape. Write `config.yaml` with: universe filters [UNIVERSE], clock definitions (time/volume/transaction), nested horizon grids {1, 5, 21, 63} trading days, half-life grid {1, 5, 21} days, evaluation horizons {1, 5, 21} days, and bucket definitions (rating × maturity-bucket × sector). Add a `Makefile` with targets: clean-data, bars, factors, evaluate. Write the scaffold, a README describing the pipeline DAG, and nothing else yet.

### P0.2 — Data acquisition
> I need a corporate-bond trade tape and reference data. Write `data/acquire.py` with three interchangeable loaders behind one interface `load_tape(source) -> DataFrame[cusip, ts, price, par_volume, side_flag, contra_party_type]`:
> 1. **WRDS TRACE Enhanced** (if I have WRDS credentials): SQL pull for [UNIVERSE], joined to Mergent FISD for amount outstanding, rating, maturity, sector.
> 2. **FINRA public TRACE** (no WRDS): document exactly what is and isn't available (dissemination caps at 5MM+/1MM+, no uncapped size) and load it.
> 3. **Synthetic generator** for end-to-end testing without any subscription: simulate 500 bonds over 3 years as marked point processes — Hawkes buy/sell arrivals with cross-excitation, prices = issuer-curve fair value + OU pressure component + impact from signed prints + noise, side flags, occasional interdealer prints. Parameterize so median bond trades ~2×/day and the 10th percentile ~2×/week.
> The synthetic generator must plant known effects (reversal after high-volume prints, sign persistence, liquid→illiquid lead-lag within issuer) so later factor tests have positive controls. Document the planted effects and their magnitudes in `data/SYNTHETIC_TRUTH.md`.

### P0.3 — TRACE cleaning
> Implement the standard TRACE Enhanced cleaning pipeline in `data/clean.py` (Dick-Nielsen style): drop cancellations and corrections by matching message sequence, reverse reversals, de-duplicate agency cross-reports and inter-dealer double counting, filter obvious price errors (median ± threshold filter and reversal filter), and normalize sides into d ∈ {+1 customer buy, −1 customer sell, 0 interdealer}. Log counts removed at each step. Unit-test each rule with hand-built message sequences. Output one parquet per month: `clean/tape_YYYYMM.parquet`.

### P0.4 — Buckets and universe
> Write `data/universe.py`: join the clean tape to reference data; construct liquidity buckets (trailing 63-day print count terciles), rating × maturity × sector buckets for cross-sectional ranking, and an issuer-curve map with the most liquid sibling per issuer (anchor bond = max trailing print count). Recompute monthly. Output `clean/universe_YYYYMM.parquet` and `clean/issuer_anchors_YYYYMM.parquet`.

### P0.5 — Clocks, bars, fallback windows (Algorithm 1)
> Implement Algorithm 1 from the compendium in `data/bars.py`. One function `make_bars(tape, clock, grid)` supporting time, volume, and transaction clocks; for each observation timestamp and each nested horizon δ ∈ {1, 5, 21, 63} days, aggregate prints with the fallback rule (shortest non-empty window; mark missing only if full history is empty), and record the **realized horizon** as a column (it is itself a staleness feature). Per bar and per side (+/−/all): count, volume, VWAP, first, last, min, max, std of prints. Property-test: bars from a permuted-order tape are identical after sorting; fallback never returns an empty window when history is non-empty.

### P0.6 — Fair-value engine (F15–F17)
> Implement `factors/fair_value.py` computing, per bond-day and per nested window with fallback: trade-price percentiles p ∈ {10,25,45,50,55,75,90} (F15), rolling min/max and range R (F16), VWAP/first/last per side (F17), and the derived series: robust fair value FV = Q50, reversal deviation (P_last − FV)/FV, dispersion Q75 − Q25, and range proxy vol R/Q50. Validate on synthetic data: FV must track the planted issuer-curve fair value with lower RMSE than last-print.

