## Phase 4 — Evaluation harness

### P4.1 — Single-factor evaluation
> Write `evaluation/single.py`: for every factor column produced in Phases 1–3, compute per-date bucket-neutral Spearman rank-IC against forward returns at h ∈ {1,5,21}d, restricted to `tradable_h` bond-days from P3.6. Report: mean IC, IC t-stat (Newey–West, lag = h), IC decay profile across h, monotonicity of decile portfolio returns, factor autocorrelation (turnover proxy), and a cost-adjusted decile spread using half of F22 as the per-trade haircut. Output one summary parquet + a one-page PDF per factor family. On synthetic data: planted factors (F10, F13, F35) must pass; a placebo (shuffled-date factor) must fail — build both checks into the harness as assertions.

### P4.2 — Redundancy and clustering
> Write `evaluation/redundancy.py`: pairwise rank correlations of all factors, hierarchical clustering, and a cluster-representative table. The compendium predicts the factors collapse to roughly one representative per section (≈11 families) — report the actual cluster count at correlation threshold 0.7 and which factors are pure duplicates.

### P4.3 — Pool combination (Algorithm 7)
> Implement Algorithm 7 in `evaluation/pool.py`: greedy admission — candidates ordered by single-factor |IC t-stat|; tentatively add, re-optimize linear weights to maximize combined rank-IC (ridge on rank-transformed factors is fine), admit only if pool IC improves AND max pairwise correlation with incumbents < 0.7; prune pass after every 5 admissions; weights frozen and refit quarterly, everything walk-forward. Report the admission order, final pool, and the pool-vs-best-single IC comparison.

### P4.4 — Liquid→illiquid transfer (Algorithm 8)
> Implement Algorithm 8 in `evaluation/transfer.py`: propagate the final pool's anchor-bond scores to illiquid siblings (with maturity-gap decay, test decay half-lives {1, 3, ∞} years of gap), plus the anchor-implied basis factor. Evaluate on illiquid-tercile bonds only. Then run the reverse direction (illiquid-fitted models on liquid bonds) purely as the falsification test — the compendium predicts it fails; report both directions side by side.

